ng_policy is a FreeBSD Netgraph node type that performs stateless,
identity-aware policy enforcement on Ethernet frames.

It registers endpoints -- each a pair of hooks, a workload side and a
fabric side -- evaluates every frame crossing one of those hooks against
a precompiled, priority-ordered rule set, and either forwards the frame
to the endpoint's other hook or drops it.

The module compiles nothing itself.  Rules, endpoint registrations and an
IPv4-to-endpoint address map arrive over its own Netgraph control socket
already in binary form, and are swapped into place atomically as a single
immutable generation under an optimistic concurrency check.  Deciding
what a rule should say -- translating tenant intent, security groups or
labels into endpoint identifiers and IPv4 rules -- is a userland concern
outside this module.

A freshly constructed node is at generation 0 with a default action of
DROP in ENFORCE mode: it is fail-closed by construction and blocks all
traffic until a policy generation is committed.
